---
description: Modett Atelier Academy project rules (stack, structure, types, errors, data safety, tests, scope)
alwaysApply: true
---

# Stack
- Monorepo: pnpm workspaces + Turborepo, Node 22, TypeScript strict. Scope: @modett
- API: NestJS (Express), Zod validation (nestjs-zod), pino logging
- DB: PostgreSQL 16 + Drizzle ORM + drizzle-kit migrations
- Redis 7 (ioredis) for sessions, locks, rate limits; BullMQ for jobs
- Tests: Jest + supertest against real Postgres and Redis (docker compose)
- Third parties (Kinescope, Airwallex, R2, email) always sit behind an adapter interface
- Deploy targets: Railway (api, worker, Postgres, Redis), Vercel (web), Cloudflare (DNS)

# Structure
- apps/api, apps/worker, packages/db, packages/shared, packages/config (apps/web and other packages are created only in their own section)
- API modules: apps/api/src/modules/<name>/ with <name>.module.ts, .controller.ts, .service.ts, .repository.ts, dto/, and tests beside the code
- Shared infrastructure: apps/api/src/infra (database, redis, queue) and apps/api/src/common (errors, guards, pipes, decorators)
- Routes are prefixed /v1. Environment variables are read only through the validated config module, never process.env elsewhere.
- Layering: controller -> service -> repository. Only repositories import Drizzle. Controllers hold no business logic.
- A module never imports another module's repository, only its exported service. No circular imports.

# Types and clean code
- strict mode. No `any`, no `@ts-ignore`, no non-null `!`. Use `unknown` and narrow with Zod. `as` only at a validated boundary with a one-line reason.
- Explicit return types on exported functions. `import type` for types. Use discriminated unions and `as const` objects instead of enums.
- Validate every input at the boundary with Zod (shared schemas live in @modett/shared).
- One responsibility per file, kebab-case names, about 300 lines per file and 40 per function max; more than 3 params means an options object.
- Money is an integer in minor units. Timestamps are UTC timestamptz.
- Use the pino logger, never console.log. Never log secrets, tokens or passwords.
- No commented-out code. Comments explain why. No TODOs unless noted in the plan.

# Errors
- Every failure is an ApiError (apps/api/src/common/errors) with a stable ErrorCode. Use its factories (badRequest, unauthorized, forbidden, notFound, conflict, unprocessable, tooManyRequests, serviceUnavailable, internal).
- Services, guards and pipes throw ApiError. Only the global exception filter writes error responses.
- NEVER write res.status(...), res.json(...) or res.send(...) for errors, and never res.status(500). Never throw new Error, HttpException or Nest built-in exceptions in feature code. Do not use @Res().
- Error body: { "error": { "code", "message", "details"?, "requestId" } }. Unknown errors become a generic INTERNAL error; log the real one with the requestId and never leak stack traces or SQL.
- Catch only to translate or add context, then rethrow an ApiError. No empty or swallowed catches. Map Postgres unique violation (23505) to conflict in the repository.

# Reads, writes and race conditions
- Reads: select explicit columns, paginate lists (default 20, max 100), no N+1. A read that informs a write happens in the same transaction.
- Writes: multi-statement writes run in one db.transaction. Never check-then-insert; rely on unique constraints with onConflictDoNothing/DoUpdate. Counters use atomic SQL. Read-modify-write uses SELECT ... FOR UPDATE or an optimistic version column.
- Webhooks, payments and queue jobs are idempotent (unique provider event ids).
- Side effects (emails, queue jobs, external calls) run after the transaction commits, never inside it.
- Redis: atomic commands only (SET NX PX, INCR, Lua), never GET then SET to decide. Locks have a TTL and an owner token.
- Timeouts on every external call; retry only idempotent operations.
- Migrations are generated by drizzle-kit, committed, additive and never edited after merge.

# Testing
- Every module ships with tests: service unit tests (repository mocked), repository tests on the real test DB, and supertest e2e (happy path, validation error, auth error, not found, conflict).
- Anything with race risk gets a concurrency test using Promise.all that asserts one winner and a consistent final state.
- Tests are deterministic: no sleeps, unique data per test. Never skip, loosen or delete a failing test.
- Before saying a task is done, run `pnpm lint && pnpm typecheck && pnpm test` and report the result.

# Scope and workflow
- Follow the prompt exactly. Do only what it asks: no extra features, files, refactors or abstractions. Ask when something is unclear, or list it under "Assumptions".
- Add a dependency only if the prompt lists it or it is strictly required; pin versions, never "latest", and state each one.
- Do not edit code outside the prompt's scope. In Plan mode do not edit files or run commands.
- Never commit secrets; .env.example holds placeholders only.
- Finish with a short report: files changed, dependencies added, assumptions, test results, anything not done.

# Product principles
- Never frustrate legitimate users. Verify an email once at signup, never on each login. Keep rate-limit waits short and escalating, with a visible countdown and a clear way out (forgot password).
- Never lock an account because of failed attempts; throttle by email plus IP instead.
- Error messages are clear and human, and never reveal whether an email has an account.
- Prices always come from the API in the visitor's region currency; never hard-code or convert prices in the client.